# Vue testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to Vue component
and composable testing with Vitest and Vue Test Utils. Applies only to
`*.vue` files' own tests; a plain `.ts` composable's unit tests follow
`ts-js-node`'s testing rule unless they mount a component.

## Layout and runner

- Co-locate a component's test next to it (`OrderSummary.vue` /
  `OrderSummary.spec.ts` or the project's existing `__tests__` convention)
  -- match whatever the project already does rather than introducing a
  second layout.
- Use the project's configured runner (Vitest is standard for Vue 3 /
  Vite projects) and its already-configured DOM environment (`happy-dom`
  or `jsdom`); do not add a second test runner or DOM environment for new
  tests.
- Use `@vue/test-utils`'s `mount()` for a component that needs its full
  render tree and child components resolved, and `shallowMount()` only
  when child components are irrelevant to the behavior under test and
  stubbing them meaningfully reduces noise.

## Querying and interaction

- Query by test id, role, or visible text (`wrapper.find('[data-testid=...]')`,
  or `@testing-library/vue`'s `getByRole`/`getByText` when the project uses
  it) instead of a CSS class or DOM structure selector that changes with
  styling.
- Trigger user interaction through Test Utils' `trigger()` (`await
  wrapper.find(selector).trigger('click')`) or `@testing-library/vue`'s
  `fireEvent`, and `await` it -- Vue's DOM updates are asynchronous, so an
  un-awaited trigger asserts against stale DOM.
- Assert on rendered output and emitted events (`wrapper.emitted('update')`),
  not on a component's internal reactive state reached through
  `wrapper.vm` -- `vm` access ties the test to implementation details that
  change without changing behavior.

## Props, emits, and stores

- Pass required props explicitly through `mount(Component, { props: {...}
  })`; do not rely on defaults silently covering a prop the test never
  sets when its value actually matters to the assertion.
- Assert an emitted event with `wrapper.emitted('eventName')` and check
  the payload array, not just that the event fired -- a wrong payload is a
  real bug an emitted-only check misses.
- For a component that uses Pinia, create a fresh test Pinia instance per
  test (`createTestingPinia()` from `@pinia/testing`, or `createPinia()` +
  `setActivePinia()`) rather than reusing one instance across tests, so
  store state from one test cannot leak into the next.

## Mocking and determinism

- Mock network calls at the request boundary (the composable/service
  function, or MSW at the fetch layer) the project already uses, not deep
  inside a component's internals.
- Use Vitest's fake timers (`vi.useFakeTimers()`) for a component that
  debounces, polls, or delays, and restore real timers afterward
  (`vi.useRealTimers()`) so later tests are not affected.
- Flush pending reactivity updates with `await nextTick()` (or the
  awaited `trigger()`/`flushPromises()` the project already imports)
  before asserting on DOM that changes after an async composable call
  resolves, instead of adding an arbitrary `setTimeout` wait.
